  "text_md": "“main” — 2025/3/10 — 1:02 — page 718 — #18\n\n718 Michael R. Douglas and Cristofero Fraser-Taliente\n\n![](<2503.05558_pg18_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n- Figure 1: Average solution length against number of nodes expanded, for 1000 initial states randomly scrambled between 1000-10000 times. We report results with a ball radius R = 5, and no T-calibration. For beam width 27 or greater, we are able to solve all states.\n\n![](<2503.05558_pg18_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\n- Figure 2: Average solution length vs beam width. We find a best fit given\n\n\nby E = 41.9log2(B)−1 − 1.92, where B is the beam width (trivially related to the number of nodes). Excesses are always even due to parity. We report results with a ball radius R = 5, and no T-calibration.\n\n",
